; The enemy status panel: up on every round rather than only after アナライズ,
; when the switch file is there.
;
; Half of a feature a build can leave out -- the other half is the switch,
; patches/enemy_info_panel.jaf, and modules/Features.js is where the pair is
; named. The same panel's two card Ids in English are a separate file,
; patches/enemy_panel_cards.jam, and no feature of anything: that is
; translation, so every build gets it.
;
; The panel is EnemyInformationView, and the game shows it from exactly one
; place: SceneBattle@OnSkillEffectPostProcess casts the finished effect to
; SkillEffectProcessShowEnemyInformation -- skill effect 48, which only
; アナライズ (skill Id 1119) carries -- and calls m_enemyInfoView.Show().
; Show() runs SetParam() and fades the panel in, and nothing ever hides it
; again, so one call per round leaves it up for the whole battle.
;
; This is a .jam rather than a .jaf because the call has to be made on a member
; of SceneBattle, and alice-tools' .jaf compiler resolves neither `this` nor a
; struct's own members inside an override -- only arguments, locals, globals
; and super(). What it can do is assemble a whole function and repoint the
; function table at it, which is what --jam is for.
;
; SceneBattle@ShowRound is the hook: it is 26 instructions, it has one caller
; (SceneBattle@StartRound, past the early return that skips a round the player
; does not get), and it runs after the battle has begun, so g_enemy -- which
; EnemyInformationView@SetParam dereferences without checking -- is set. The
; body below is that function verbatim, from
;   alice ain dump -c game/ain/Rance10.v1.04.ain
; with the tail added. Regenerate it the same way if the game version changes:
; every number here is an index into one build of one .ain.
;
; The switch itself is patches/enemy_info_panel.jaf, not this file. It builds a
; path, and a path needs a string local -- which cannot go here, because --jam
; rewrites code and not the FUNC section, so this function's three locals are
; the three it was compiled with. CALLFUNC resolves EnemyInfoPanelEnabled by
; name, which means alice-tools has to be handed that .jaf *before* this file:
; the other order stops with "Unable to resolve function".
;
; Save this file as UTF-8 with no BOM. The assembler reads a byte order mark as
; an instruction and stops on line 1 -- "Invalid instruction: ϻ" -- and an
; editor that defaults .jam to UTF-8-with-BOM will quietly put one back.
;
;   CALLFUNC EnemyInfoPanelEnabled           is the switch file there
;   IFZ skip_panel                           no: leave the panel alone
;   .STRUCTREF SceneBattle m_enemyInfoView   the panel
;   PUSH 24858                               EnemyInformationView@Show
;   CALLMETHOD 0                             no arguments
;
; Showing it once per round rather than once per battle also refreshes it: the
; enemy can change mid-battle (SceneBattle@OnInsertionEnemyChanged), and the
; panel would otherwise keep describing the enemy that is gone. The cost is
; that Show()'s slide-in animation replays each round.
FUNC 24905
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 24944
	.LOCALREF "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	DELETE
	PUSHLOCALPAGE
	PUSH 0
	.STRUCTREF SceneBattle m_context
	PUSH 26574
	CALLMETHOD 0
	.STACK_LOCALASSIGN "<dummy : BattleContext@Round::getの戻り値>"
	NEW BattleLog 27222
	ASSIGN
	CALLMETHOD 1
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : new BattleLog>"
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : BattleContext@Round::getの戻り値>"
	.STRUCTREF SceneBattle m_roundView
	PUSH 24832
	.STRUCTREF SceneBattle m_context
	PUSH 26574
	CALLMETHOD 0
	.STACK_LOCALASSIGN "<dummy : BattleContext@Round::getの戻り値>#1"
	PUSH 26653
	CALLMETHOD 0
	PUSH 1
	CALLMETHOD 2
	.LOCALDELETE "<dummy : BattleContext@Round::getの戻り値>#1"
	CALLFUNC EnemyInfoPanelEnabled
	IFZ skip_panel
	.STRUCTREF SceneBattle m_enemyInfoView
	PUSH 24858
	CALLMETHOD 0
skip_panel:
	RETURN
ENDFUNC SceneBattle@ShowRound
